# GOLDEN FIXTURE — a NEUTRALIZED copy of a consumer repo's `.assay-versions` file.
#
# This is the load-bearing backward-compat fixture for the pin reader/validator:
# every backward-compat assertion in the pin-file spec runs against THIS file. It is
# shaped like a real, comment-heavy consumer pin file so the reader must never break
# on the properties a live one carries:
#   * comment-heavy, with blank lines and `#` header/section blocks;
#   * `<artifact>-<platform>` per-platform naming;
#   * trailing `# ...` comments after field 3;
#   * `statusgen` and `statusgen-linux-amd64` carrying an IDENTICAL tag+sha256
#     (distinct names, NOT a duplicate);
#   * desk-tools pinned PER-PLATFORM ONLY — there is no bare `desk-tools` line, so a
#     bare lookup must fail-closed rather than match a per-platform line.
#
# NEUTRALIZED, not verbatim. `tools/desk/` is copy-dispositioned into the public tree
# (docs/publication-manifest.yaml), so the comment prose here is written in sanctioned
# public forms only — no private org/repo slugs, product/infra short-names, or
# internal issue citations. The comments are inert to the parser, so neutralizing them
# changes NO parsed content: the 12 artifact lines below (and their trailing inline
# comments) are the real assertions and are preserved exactly. Refresh by re-copying a
# live consumer file and re-applying the same neutralization, keeping the artifact
# lines byte-identical, and updating the Source revision line below to the new commit id.
#
# Source revision: 33fa2723ff55355df4aa0f2c81361895e0ef8952 — the live consumer's
# `.assay-versions` at that commit. The source repo slug is withheld (it is the leak the
# neutralization removes); a bare 40-hex commit id carries no private identifier and is
# safe in the public tree. This anchors WHICH live revision the backward-compat
# assertions are compatible with, so drift between this fixture and the live file stays
# detectable.
#
# One normalization vs a raw copy: a single trailing newline at end-of-file, so the
# append-based duplicate-rejection test forms a new line instead of gluing onto the
# final comment. That changes no parsed content.
# =========================================================================================
# Pinned release artifacts -- tag + sha256 per artifact. Consumers verify the
# downloaded binary against the hash committed here, so a re-tagged release cannot
# silently substitute a binary.
#
# Format: <artifact> <tag> <sha256>
#
# Cross-org access to the private release repo (medici-finance/assay) is via a
# runtime-generated GitHub App installation token; each CI workflow mints a
# short-lived token before downloading the pinned binary. For a local install, the
# invoking user's `gh` auth supplies credentials.

statusgen statusgen/v0.8.2 654d0958984fb2ec483f65c459a87fe0801785126b3624cdf981b6cc644c78e3  # linux-amd64 (CI-built)

# Per-platform statusgen pins, same three-field shape as desk-tools below. The bare
# `statusgen` line ABOVE stays as-is: workflows select their pin with
# `grep '^statusgen '` (trailing space), which never matches `statusgen-<platform>` —
# so these lines are purely additive and change no CI behaviour. They exist because a
# local install selects by DETECTED platform and needs a committed hash per platform,
# rather than re-deriving trust from the release's own checksums.txt (which ships FROM
# the release being checked). Hashes are harvested from the PUBLISHED release, never a
# local build (a local build lacks the release `-ldflags` stamp and hashes differently).
statusgen-linux-amd64  statusgen/v0.8.2 654d0958984fb2ec483f65c459a87fe0801785126b3624cdf981b6cc644c78e3  # CI runners
statusgen-darwin-arm64 statusgen/v0.8.2 b24708402f098d67e0f32e5109412014ca5a547a448cd4a2952502859f31c4f7  # primary dev
statusgen-darwin-amd64 statusgen/v0.8.2 ecf5b92d683c1a23aa28aa0e0a8bf35e7599767f56a446a64a094d8aff047d23  # intel mac

# daily-harvest — the scheduled daily collector, a statusgen consumer pinned in the
# same four-line shape: a bare `daily-harvest ` line CI selects with
# `grep '^daily-harvest '` (trailing space, never matching `daily-harvest-<platform>`),
# plus per-platform lines for local installs. Only the daily-harvest workflow consumes
# these today, and it always wants linux-amd64. Hashes harvested from the PUBLISHED
# release, never a local build — Go stamps VCS provenance into the binary, so a local
# build of identical source hashes differently.
daily-harvest              daily-harvest/v0.1.0 236abf9ff609607bb1c7fa7d5fb3114ed1db0d1e043776186e5ececabbb25077  # linux-amd64 (CI-built)
daily-harvest-linux-amd64  daily-harvest/v0.1.0 236abf9ff609607bb1c7fa7d5fb3114ed1db0d1e043776186e5ececabbb25077  # CI runners
daily-harvest-darwin-arm64 daily-harvest/v0.1.0 cca6a8fa5b654bebe79fdc3a9342cd3a6c5b447ecd7921058fcc5193ad6b2f23  # primary dev
daily-harvest-darwin-amd64 daily-harvest/v0.1.0 315b51366f1c1ade8c650c3ae94eadc4673d4c40781603281dd8a967099b5fa6  # intel mac

# desk-tools: the tarball is PLATFORM-SPECIFIC, so the pin is too — one line per
# platform, artifact name `desk-tools-<platform>`, same three-field shape. The platform
# is detected at install time or fixed by the runner image (CI, always linux-amd64).
# All three hashes are pinned deliberately: a hash committed here is the only thing a
# re-tagged release cannot silently substitute, and CI (linux-amd64) would otherwise
# have no committed hash to verify against. The pinned value is the sha256 of the
# `.tar.gz` asset, harvested from the PUBLISHED release and corroborated against the
# release's own checksums.txt.
desk-tools-linux-amd64  desk-tools/v0.2.6 7fdc4a571c5932c2d1db0087c1dd9252d26cf600ba28c69359bd375fda9620d1  # CI runners
desk-tools-darwin-arm64 desk-tools/v0.2.6 867eb7d4f31ef4910d44152f47580dcb19e65e10641c3894b1f6bac242069c6a  # primary dev
desk-tools-darwin-amd64 desk-tools/v0.2.6 112dbb75e3e1f1342992f8c55886d42e80afb2696a088a6a7474032c8e063d27  # intel mac

# THE SOURCE PIN. The three lines above pin the BINARIES by sha256. This one pins the
# SOURCE tree, because the consumer action also materialises the `tools/desk` tree so
# the consumer-tagged structural tests can compile and run it — and a tag is a mutable
# reference. Field 3 is a 40-hex COMMIT sha (not a 64-hex asset digest): the same role,
# a different object. It uses the same `<artifact> <tag> <field3>` grammar, selected by
# `grep '^desk-tools-source '`, so no existing per-platform selector can match it —
# purely additive. The action verifies the clone's `git rev-parse HEAD` against this
# value before any of it reaches the workspace, and refuses if this tag differs from the
# binaries' tag, so a half-moved bump is red rather than silently skewed. On the next
# bump, move this line WITH the three binary pins above, in the same commit.
desk-tools-source       desk-tools/v0.2.6 abad1deaabad1deaabad1deaabad1deaabad1dea  # tools/desk tree compiled by gate-pins

# NOTE: this fixture carries NO umbrella line, so the umbrella reader's
# absent-is-a-valid-third-state path is exercised against it. An umbrella line, when
# present, is a two-field `<umbrella> <tag>` (no digest); a digest on it is malformed.
